module ECL_CONCURRENCY.

import ECL_UNITS.

context Concurrency.
context Mailboxes.

> This model is normative for structured task identity, attachment to the
> general unit lifecycle, task ownership, cached terminal results, observation
> by `task.await`, deadline expiry, cancellation, the requirement that a
> task quiesce the tasks it owns before its attached unit becomes terminal,
> and the lifecycle of the messages a task's mailbox holds.
>
> It abstracts over evaluator steps, scheduling policy, elapsed time, I/O
> interleaving, handle storage and lexical scope, and the selection algorithms
> used by `task.await-any`, sequential awaits, and `@each`. The surrounding language
> specification governs those operations. A conforming scheduler may run
> runnable tasks serially; the model promises lifetime and result semantics,
> without promising simultaneous progress or fairness.
> Task capabilities are immutable ECL values and may be shared. The mutable
> evaluation state of each task remains confined to its attached unit.
>
> Task scopes exist at exactly two places: the session root and each spawned
> task. A spawn is owned by the task whose attached unit is executing it, or
> by the session root when no task is executing. Other units, such as
> `@attempt`, combinator applications, `load`, and module construction, are
> stack transactions and never owners: a task spawned inside one belongs to
> the enclosing scope and is unaffected by that unit's success or failure.
> The session root closes only at `exit` or session end, which this model
> leaves outside its actions.
>
> A created task permanently names one attached unit and is owned either by
> the session root or by one other task. Its capability is an ECL value of the
> task kind. No two task identities attach to the same unit or expose the same
> capability value. `task-owner` is meaningful only for a task that is not
> root-owned; every proposition that reads it is guarded accordingly.
Task.
{Concurrency} task-created? task: Task => Bool.
{Concurrency} task-unit task: Task => Unit.
{Concurrency} root-owned? task: Task => Bool.
{Concurrency} task-owner task: Task => Task.
task-capability task: Task => Value.
> Every task owns one mailbox, open exactly while its attached unit is active.
> A message is created by `task.send` addressed to one task and is queued
> until that task receives it or its mailbox closes. Message payloads,
> capacity and parking, per-sender order, and the handle check on send are
> governed by the surrounding specification; the model carries only
> existence, target, and the queued/received/discarded lifecycle.
Message.
{Mailboxes} message-created? message: Message => Bool.
{Mailboxes} message-target message: Message => Task.
{Mailboxes} received? message: Message => Bool.
{Mailboxes} discarded? message: Message => Bool.
---
> Every created task has a task-valued capability and attaches to a created
> unit. A task not owned by the root is owned by a distinct created task.
all task: Task, task-created? task |
  value-type (task-capability task) = task-type and
  unit-created? (task-unit task).

all task: Task, task-created? task, ~root-owned? task |
  task-created? (task-owner task) and
  task-owner task != task.

> Attached units are unique to their task identities.
all left: Task, right: Task,
    task-created? left,
    task-created? right,
    task-unit left = task-unit right |
  left = right.

> Created tasks have distinct capability values.
all left: Task, right: Task,
    task-created? left,
    task-created? right,
    task-capability left = task-capability right |
  left = right.

> A created message addresses a created task and is in at most one terminal
> state. A message is queued when it is created and neither received nor
> discarded; a queued message's target is still active, because closing a
> mailbox discards everything it holds.
all message: Message, message-created? message |
  task-created? (message-target message) and
  ~(received? message and discarded? message).

all message: Message,
    message-created? message,
    ~received? message,
    ~discarded? message |
  unit-active? (task-unit (message-target message)).

> Initially no task identity or message has been created.
initially all task: Task |
  ~task-created? task.

initially all message: Message |
  ~message-created? message.

where

> `Spawn task under task` and `Spawn task under root` are the formal `@spawn`
> boundary. The explicit values list and quotation body become the attached
> unit's complete entry stack and body. Ambient operands are absent from the
> action, so they cannot cross the boundary. The action chooses both a fresh
> task identity and a fresh unit identity, attaches them permanently, and
> records the owning task or the session root.
Units, Concurrency ~> Spawn task under task @ owner: Task, entry: Value, body: Value, task-created? owner, unit-active? (task-unit owner), ~unit-succeeded? (task-unit owner), ~unit-failed? (task-unit owner), value-type entry = list-type, value-type body = list-type.
---
> Spawning creates one task and one attached active unit. Both identities are
> fresh, the owner is recorded, and all previously created task and unit state
> is preserved.
some task: Task, child: Unit,
    ~task-created? task,
    ~unit-created? child |
  task-created?' task and
  task-unit' task = child and
  ~root-owned?' task and
  task-owner' task = owner and
  value-type (task-capability task) = task-type and
  (all existing: Task, task-created? existing |
    task-capability task != task-capability existing) and
  unit-created?' child and
  unit-active?' child and
  ~unit-succeeded?' child and
  ~unit-failed?' child and
  unit-entry' child = entry and
  unit-body' child = body and
  unit-stack' child = entry and
  unit-error' child = unit-error child and
  (all other-task: Task, other-task != task |
    task-created?' other-task = task-created? other-task and
    task-unit' other-task = task-unit other-task and
    root-owned?' other-task = root-owned? other-task) and
  (all other-task: Task, other-task != task |
    task-owner' other-task = task-owner other-task) and
  (all other-unit: Unit, other-unit != child |
    unit-created?' other-unit = unit-created? other-unit and
    unit-active?' other-unit = unit-active? other-unit and
    unit-succeeded?' other-unit = unit-succeeded? other-unit and
    unit-failed?' other-unit = unit-failed? other-unit and
    unit-entry' other-unit = unit-entry other-unit and
    unit-body' other-unit = unit-body other-unit and
    unit-stack' other-unit = unit-stack other-unit and
    unit-error' other-unit = unit-error other-unit).

where

> `Spawn task under root` is the same boundary when no task is executing:
> a root unit, or a stack transaction inside one, spawns into the session
> root scope.
Units, Concurrency ~> Spawn task under root @ entry: Value, body: Value, value-type entry = list-type, value-type body = list-type.
---
some task: Task, child: Unit,
    ~task-created? task,
    ~unit-created? child |
  task-created?' task and
  task-unit' task = child and
  root-owned?' task and
  value-type (task-capability task) = task-type and
  (all existing: Task, task-created? existing |
    task-capability task != task-capability existing) and
  unit-created?' child and
  unit-active?' child and
  ~unit-succeeded?' child and
  ~unit-failed?' child and
  unit-entry' child = entry and
  unit-body' child = body and
  unit-stack' child = entry and
  unit-error' child = unit-error child and
  (all other-task: Task, other-task != task |
    task-created?' other-task = task-created? other-task and
    task-unit' other-task = task-unit other-task and
    root-owned?' other-task = root-owned? other-task) and
  (all other-task: Task, other-task != task |
    task-owner' other-task = task-owner other-task) and
  (all other-unit: Unit, other-unit != child |
    unit-created?' other-unit = unit-created? other-unit and
    unit-active?' other-unit = unit-active? other-unit and
    unit-succeeded?' other-unit = unit-succeeded? other-unit and
    unit-failed?' other-unit = unit-failed? other-unit and
    unit-entry' other-unit = unit-entry other-unit and
    unit-body' other-unit = unit-body other-unit and
    unit-stack' other-unit = unit-stack other-unit and
    unit-error' other-unit = unit-error other-unit).

where

> A task's terminal result is a single immutable ECL value. A successful
> attached unit yields the exact ordered terminal stack in an `'ok` envelope;
> a failed attached unit yields its unchanged error value in an `'err`
> envelope. Because `task-result` is immutable, every observation of a
> terminal task produces the same value.
task-result task: Task => Value.
---
> Successful tasks have successful result envelopes.
all task: Task,
    task-created? task,
    unit-succeeded? (task-unit task) |
  successful-result-value? (task-result task).

> The successful result payload is exactly the attached unit's terminal stack
> list value.
all task: Task,
    task-created? task,
    unit-succeeded? (task-unit task) |
  successful-result-values (task-result task) =
    unit-stack (task-unit task).

> Failed tasks have failed result envelopes containing the unit's exact error
> value.
all task: Task,
    task-created? task,
    unit-failed? (task-unit task) |
  failed-result-value? (task-result task).

all task: Task,
    task-created? task,
    unit-failed? (task-unit task) |
  failed-result-error (task-result task) =
    unit-error (task-unit task).

> A task's attached unit may become terminal only after every task it owns is
> terminal. Applying the same rule recursively gives structured quiescence for
> the complete descendant task tree. This is the task-scope part of the rule
> that a failed task leaves nothing running.
all owner: Task,
    task-created? owner,
    unit-succeeded? (task-unit owner) or unit-failed? (task-unit owner) |
  all task: Task,
      task-created? task,
      ~root-owned? task,
      task-owner task = owner |
    unit-succeeded? (task-unit task) or unit-failed? (task-unit task).

where

> `Send` is the formal `task.send`: it creates one message addressed to an
> active task. Sending to a task whose
> attached unit is terminal fails and creates nothing; the surrounding
> specification names that failure. Parking under pressure is outside the
> model.
Mailboxes ~> Send @ target: Task, task-created? target, unit-active? (task-unit target).
---
some message: Message, ~message-created? message |
  message-created?' message and
  message-target' message = target and
  ~received?' message and
  ~discarded?' message and
  (all other: Message, other != message |
    message-created?' other = message-created? other and
    message-target' other = message-target other and
    received?' other = received? other and
    discarded?' other = discarded? other).

where

> `Receive` is the formal `task.receive`: the receiving task takes one queued
> message addressed to it. Which queued message is taken is governed by the
> surrounding specification's ordering rule.
Mailboxes ~> Receive @ receiver: Task, message: Message, task-created? receiver, unit-active? (task-unit receiver), message-created? message, message-target message = receiver, ~received? message, ~discarded? message.
---
received?' message.
~discarded?' message.
message-created?' message.
message-target' message = message-target message.

all other: Message, other != message |
  message-created?' other = message-created? other and
  message-target' other = message-target other and
  received?' other = received? other and
  discarded?' other = discarded? other.

where

> `Complete spawned task successfully` is the concurrency-layer use of the
> general unit success transition. The evaluator supplies the final stack.
> The attached unit can become terminal only after its own task scope has
> quiesced.
Units, Concurrency, Mailboxes ~> Complete spawned task successfully @ task: Task, result: Value, task-created? task, unit-active? (task-unit task), value-type result = list-type, (all child: Task, task-created? child, ~root-owned? child, task-owner child = task | unit-succeeded? (task-unit child) or unit-failed? (task-unit child)).
---
unit-created?' (task-unit task).
~unit-active?' (task-unit task).
unit-succeeded?' (task-unit task).
~unit-failed?' (task-unit task).
unit-entry' (task-unit task) = unit-entry (task-unit task).
unit-body' (task-unit task) = unit-body (task-unit task).
unit-stack' (task-unit task) = result.
unit-error' (task-unit task) = unit-error (task-unit task).
successful-result-value? (task-result task).
successful-result-values (task-result task) = result.

all other: Unit, other != task-unit task |
  unit-created?' other = unit-created? other and
  unit-active?' other = unit-active? other and
  unit-succeeded?' other = unit-succeeded? other and
  unit-failed?' other = unit-failed? other and
  unit-entry' other = unit-entry other and
  unit-body' other = unit-body other and
  unit-stack' other = unit-stack other and
  unit-error' other = unit-error other.

all candidate: Task |
  task-created?' candidate = task-created? candidate and
  task-unit' candidate = task-unit candidate and
  root-owned?' candidate = root-owned? candidate.

all candidate: Task |
  task-owner' candidate = task-owner candidate.

> Becoming terminal closes the mailbox: every message still queued for this
> task is discarded, and nothing else about messages changes.
all message: Message,
    message-created? message,
    message-target message = task,
    ~received? message |
  discarded?' message.

all message: Message,
    ~(message-created? message and message-target message = task and ~received? message) |
  discarded?' message = discarded? message.

all message: Message |
  message-created?' message = message-created? message and
  message-target' message = message-target message and
  received?' message = received? message.

where

> `Complete spawned task with failure` is the concurrency-layer use of the
> general unit failure transition. The evaluator supplies the error. The
> attached unit restores its entry stack and becomes terminal after its own
> task scope has quiesced.
Units, Concurrency, Mailboxes ~> Complete spawned task with failure @ task: Task, error: Value, task-created? task, unit-active? (task-unit task), error-value? error, (all child: Task, task-created? child, ~root-owned? child, task-owner child = task | unit-succeeded? (task-unit child) or unit-failed? (task-unit child)).
---
unit-created?' (task-unit task).
~unit-active?' (task-unit task).
~unit-succeeded?' (task-unit task).
unit-failed?' (task-unit task).
unit-entry' (task-unit task) = unit-entry (task-unit task).
unit-body' (task-unit task) = unit-body (task-unit task).
unit-stack' (task-unit task) = unit-entry (task-unit task).
unit-error' (task-unit task) = error.
failed-result-value? (task-result task).
failed-result-error (task-result task) = error.

all other: Unit, other != task-unit task |
  unit-created?' other = unit-created? other and
  unit-active?' other = unit-active? other and
  unit-succeeded?' other = unit-succeeded? other and
  unit-failed?' other = unit-failed? other and
  unit-entry' other = unit-entry other and
  unit-body' other = unit-body other and
  unit-stack' other = unit-stack other and
  unit-error' other = unit-error other.

all candidate: Task |
  task-created?' candidate = task-created? candidate and
  task-unit' candidate = task-unit candidate and
  root-owned?' candidate = root-owned? candidate.

all candidate: Task |
  task-owner' candidate = task-owner candidate.

> Becoming terminal closes the mailbox: every message still queued for this
> task is discarded, and nothing else about messages changes.
all message: Message,
    message-created? message,
    message-target message = task,
    ~received? message |
  discarded?' message.

all message: Message,
    ~(message-created? message and message-target message = task and ~received? message) |
  discarded?' message = discarded? message.

all message: Message |
  message-created?' message = message-created? message and
  message-target' message = message-target message and
  received?' message = received? message.

where

> `Await task` observes only a terminal attached unit and leaves both unit and
> task state unchanged. It yields `task-result task`; repeating the action is
> therefore safe and produces the cached result again. Parking and resumption
> before the terminal observation are scheduling details outside the model.
Concurrency ~> Await task @ observer: Unit, task: Task, unit-created? observer, unit-active? observer, task-created? task, unit-succeeded? (task-unit task) or unit-failed? (task-unit task).
---
all candidate: Task |
  task-created?' candidate = task-created? candidate and
  task-unit' candidate = task-unit candidate and
  root-owned?' candidate = root-owned? candidate.

all candidate: Task |
  task-owner' candidate = task-owner candidate.

where

> `Await task until deadline expires` is the timeout branch of `task.await-for`.
> It is available only while the attached unit is active, yields a value
> satisfying `timeout-result?`, and leaves the task and attached unit unchanged. A
> terminal task therefore wins over deadline expiry, including at a zero
> deadline. Deadline measurement and parking are outside the model.
Concurrency ~> Await task until deadline expires @ observer: Unit, task: Task, unit-created? observer, unit-active? observer, task-created? task, unit-active? (task-unit task).
---
all candidate: Task |
  task-created?' candidate = task-created? candidate and
  task-unit' candidate = task-unit candidate and
  root-owned?' candidate = root-owned? candidate.

all candidate: Task |
  task-owner' candidate = task-owner candidate.

where

> `Cancel active task` is ordinary unit failure with a cancellation error. It
> restores the unit's entry stack and becomes terminal only after the task's
> own descendants are quiescent.
Units, Concurrency, Mailboxes ~> Cancel active task @ task: Task, error: Value, task-created? task, unit-active? (task-unit task), cancelled-error? error, (all child: Task, task-created? child, ~root-owned? child, task-owner child = task | unit-succeeded? (task-unit child) or unit-failed? (task-unit child)).
---
unit-created?' (task-unit task).
~unit-active?' (task-unit task).
~unit-succeeded?' (task-unit task).
unit-failed?' (task-unit task).
unit-entry' (task-unit task) = unit-entry (task-unit task).
unit-body' (task-unit task) = unit-body (task-unit task).
unit-stack' (task-unit task) = unit-entry (task-unit task).
unit-error' (task-unit task) = error.
failed-result-value? (task-result task).
failed-result-error (task-result task) = error.

all other: Unit, other != task-unit task |
  unit-created?' other = unit-created? other and
  unit-active?' other = unit-active? other and
  unit-succeeded?' other = unit-succeeded? other and
  unit-failed?' other = unit-failed? other and
  unit-entry' other = unit-entry other and
  unit-body' other = unit-body other and
  unit-stack' other = unit-stack other and
  unit-error' other = unit-error other.

all candidate: Task |
  task-created?' candidate = task-created? candidate and
  task-unit' candidate = task-unit candidate and
  root-owned?' candidate = root-owned? candidate.

all candidate: Task |
  task-owner' candidate = task-owner candidate.

> Becoming terminal closes the mailbox: every message still queued for this
> task is discarded, and nothing else about messages changes.
all message: Message,
    message-created? message,
    message-target message = task,
    ~received? message |
  discarded?' message.

all message: Message,
    ~(message-created? message and message-target message = task and ~received? message) |
  discarded?' message = discarded? message.

all message: Message |
  message-created?' message = message-created? message and
  message-target' message = message-target message and
  received?' message = received? message.

where

> Cancelling a terminal task is a no-op. Its cached result remains unchanged.
Units, Concurrency ~> Cancel terminal task @ task: Task, task-created? task, unit-succeeded? (task-unit task) or unit-failed? (task-unit task).
---
all candidate: Unit |
  unit-created?' candidate = unit-created? candidate and
  unit-active?' candidate = unit-active? candidate and
  unit-succeeded?' candidate = unit-succeeded? candidate and
  unit-failed?' candidate = unit-failed? candidate and
  unit-entry' candidate = unit-entry candidate and
  unit-body' candidate = unit-body candidate and
  unit-stack' candidate = unit-stack candidate and
  unit-error' candidate = unit-error candidate.

all candidate: Task |
  task-created?' candidate = task-created? candidate and
  task-unit' candidate = task-unit candidate and
  root-owned?' candidate = root-owned? candidate.

all candidate: Task |
  task-owner' candidate = task-owner candidate.
